#### DvannaDvol

The second-order partial derivative of delta with respect to volatility, also known as DvannaDvol, is given by

\[\frac{\partial^{3}c}{\partial S\partial\sigma^{2}} =\frac{\partial^{3}p}{\partial S\partial\sigma^{2}}=\frac{-e^{(b-r )T}d_{2}}{\sigma}n(d_{1})\frac{1}{\sigma}\left(d_{1}d_{2}-\frac{d_{1}}{d_{2}} -1\right)\] \[=\text{vanna}\left(\frac{1}{\sigma}\right)\left(d_{1}d_{2}-\frac {d_{1}}{d_{2}}-1\right) \tag{2.7}\]

It is necessary to divide by 10,000 to get this Greek on the metric of a one point change in volatility--for example, from 20% to 21%.

Figure 5 illustrates DvannaDvol for varying asset price and time to maturity.

#### 2.1.7 DdeltaDtime, Charm

DdeltatDtime, also known as charm (Garman, 1992) or Delta Bleed, a term used in the excellent book by Taleb (1997), measures the sensitivity of delta to changes in time,

\[-\frac{\partial\Delta_{\rm call}}{\partial T} =-e^{(b-r)T}\bigg{[}n(d_{1})\left(\frac{b}{\sigma\sqrt{T}}-\frac{ d_{2}}{2T}\right)\] \[\quad+(b-r)N(d_{1})\bigg{]}\leq\geq 0 \tag{2.8}\]

and

\[-\frac{\partial\Delta_{\rm put}}{\partial T} =-e^{(b-r)T}\bigg{[}n(d_{1})\left(\frac{b}{\sigma\sqrt{T}}-\frac{ d_{2}}{2T}\right)\] \[\quad-(b-r)N(-d_{1})\bigg{]}\leq\geq 0 \tag{2.9}\]

This Greek indicates what happens with delta when we move closer to maturity. Figure 6 illustrates the charm value for different values of the underlying asset and different times to maturity.

