For practical purposes, where one typically wants to look at DgammaDvol for a one-unit volatility change--for example, from 30% to 31%--one should divide the DgammaDvol by 100. Moreover, DgammaDvol and DgammaPDvol are negative for asset prices between \(S_{L}\) and \(S_{U}\) and positive outside this interval, where

