##### Bleed-Offset Volatility

A more practical relationship between theta and vega is what is known as bleed-offset volatility. It measures how much the volatility must increase to offset the theta-bleed/time decay. Bleed-offset volatility can be found simply by dividing the one-day theta by vega, \(\frac{\Theta}{\text{Vega}}\). In the case of positive theta, you can actually have negative offset volatility. Deep-in-the-money European options can have positive theta, and in this case the offset volatility will be negative.

