### Application

Exchange options are embedded in several financial contracts. One example is when a corporation bids on another corporation by offering its own shares in exchange for the stocks in the takeover candidate. The owners of the takeover candidate receive an option to exchange their stocks for the stocks in the acquiring corporation.

There are also exchange options that are embedded in short bond or note futures contracts. If you are short a bond future, you have to deliver a bond to the counterparty that is long the future at the expiration of the future's contract. However, the counterparty that is short the future can normally choose from a whole class of bonds to deliver. Such bonds can be valued as exchange options.

