#### The GARCH Process

Let us assume the variance in a continuous version is defined by the GARCH(1,1) process:

\[dv=\kappa(\theta-v)dt+\gamma v\;dX,\]

where \(v\) is the variance, \(\kappa\) is the speed of mean reversion, \(\theta\) is the mean reversion level, and \(\gamma\) is the volatility of volatility, or more precisely, the volatility of the square of volatility. The discrete version of the GARCH(1,1) process is described in Engle and Mezrich (1995) as

\[v_{n+1}=(1-\alpha-\beta)V+\alpha u_{n}^{2}+\beta v_{n},\]

where \(V\) is the long-term variance, \(u_{n}\) is the drift adjusted stock return at time \(n\), \(\alpha\) is the weight assigned to \(u_{n}^{2}\), and \(\beta\) is the weight assigned to \(v_{n}\).7

Footnote 7: The GARCH(1,1) model implies that the stock price process and the volatility process contain two uncorrelated Brownian motions. In an NGARCH process, described by Engle and Ng (1993), we have

\[v_{n+1}=(1-\alpha-\beta)V+\alpha(u_{n}-c)^{2}+\beta v_{n}\]

 where \(c\) is another parameter to be estimated and creates the correlation between the two processes. This will not, however, affect the results we present, and we concentrate on the GARCH(1,1). The NGARCH process is discussed, for instance, in Ritchken and Trevor (1997).

\[v_{n+1}=(1-\alpha-\beta)V+\alpha(u_{n}-c)^{2}+\beta v_{n}\]

where \(v\) is the variance, \(\kappa\) is the speed of mean reversion, \(\theta\) is the mean reversion level, and \(\gamma\) is the volatility of volatility, or more precisely, the volatility of the square of volatility. The discrete version of the GARCH(1,1) process is described in Engle and Mezrich (1995) as

\[v_{n+1}=(1-\alpha-\beta)V+\alpha u_{n}^{2}+\beta v_{n},\]

where \(V\) is the long-term variance, \(u_{n}\) is the drift adjusted stock return at time \(n\), \(\alpha\) is the weight assigned to \(u_{n}^{2}\), and \(\beta\) is the weight assigned to \(v_{n}\).8

Footnote 8: The \(\gamma\) in the Engle and Mezrich (1995) paper is \(\gamma=\alpha\sqrt{(\xi-1)dt}\), which is different from the one presented here. This is likely due to a small typo in their paper. See also Nelson (1990). We moreover have the following relationship

\[\theta=\frac{V}{dt}\]

\[\kappa=\frac{1-\alpha-\beta}{dt}\]

\[\gamma=\alpha\sqrt{\frac{\xi-1}{dt}},\]

where9\(\xi\) is the Pearson kurtosis (fourth moment) of \(u[n]\).

Footnote 9: The \(\gamma\) in the Engle and Mezrich (1995) paper is \(\gamma=\alpha\sqrt{(\xi-1)dt}\), which is different from the one presented here. This is likely due to a small typo in their paper. See also Nelson (1990).

